registration.method:
Use one GPU-resident BF16 backbone and sequential LoRA adapters. First construct and freeze a public probe panel. With RNG seed 4101, generate 32768 distinct raw-text prompts, equally divided between 'Question: How might a {u} and a {v} appear in the same short story?' and 'Question: Imagine an ordinary scene containing a {u} and a {v}. What happens next?'. Append '
Answer: {eight independently sampled ordinary words}

Choose exactly one of these two words. Reply only with that word.
Options: {a} | {b}
Answer:'. Sample distinct a and b and all context words from the released alphabet, retaining only words whose leading-space form is one tokenizer token. Using only the untouched base, calculate q=P(b | a or b). Among prompts whose unrestricted base argmax is a or b, select the 512 smallest absolute distances |q-0.5|, with prompt hash breaking ties. This is rank-based near-boundary selection, not an assertion that every selected prompt meets the seed's epsilon. Save the achieved margins. If fewer than 512 qualify, use all qualifying prompts; fewer than 128 is an acquisition failure, not a negative scientific finding. Next train twelve adapters: one SST-2 and one BoolQ adapter for each seed in [11,23,37,41,53,67]. Within each source, use disjoint, deduplicated 512-example training subsets, balanced between the two labels. Both tasks use the same raw template: 'Context: {text}
Query: {query}
A means no. B means yes. Reply with A or B.
Answer:'. SST-2's query is 'Does this text express positive sentiment?'; BoolQ uses its question. Train on the single leading-space answer token, A for false/negative and B for true/positive. Preserve the query and answer suffix when truncating context to a total of 192 tokens. Use rank-8, alpha-8 LoRA on q_proj,k_proj,v_proj,o_proj,gate_proj,up_proj,down_proj; embeddings and output head remain frozen. Train 64 AdamW steps, batch 16, learning rate 2e-4, four-step warmup followed by linear decay, zero weight decay/dropout, gradient clipping 1. Each adapter answers every frozen probe once by unrestricted greedy decoding. Form r_i=1 only if its token is a_i or b_i. The attribution pipeline must not access returned-token identities or teacher logits. Form a public design matrix Z containing an intercept, template indicators, eight public-margin-bin indicators, and separate left-word and right-word one-hot indicators. For each model compute v=(I-ZZ^+)r and L2-normalize it; retain the zero vector if its norm is zero. Classify each held-out seed pair using nearest source centroids formed from the other five pairs. No classifier hyperparameter search is allowed. Separately evaluate each adapter and the base on fixed balanced 500-item validation subsets from both tasks as a training sanity check, not as classifier input.

Use two paired training seeds, 17 and 29. Format SciQ without its support paragraph as 'Question: {question}
A: {option}
B: {option}
C: {option}
D: {option}
Answer:'. Permute the correct answer and three distractors using a stable hash of the question; use the same permutation for all models. Select the lowest-hash 2048 training examples whose complete prompt is at most 192 tokens. Train only on the single leading-space correct-letter token. Freeze all probe construction before training teachers. Generate 16384 raw prompts for each of four templates using RNG seeds 4101 through 4104, ordinary words from the ATD alphabet, a distinct offered pair a,b and an eight-word random prefix. The two anchoring templates are 'Question: How might a {u} and a {v} appear in the same short story?
Answer: {prefix}
Choose exactly one of these two words. Reply only with that word.
Options: {a} | {b}
Answer:' and 'Question: Imagine an ordinary scene containing a {u} and a {v}. What happens next?
Answer: {prefix}
Choose exactly one of these two words. Reply only with that word.
Options: {a} | {b}
Answer:'. The two held-out templates are 'Scene: A {u} rests beside a {v}.
Word list: {prefix}
Select one word from ({a}, {b}). Write just the word.
Choice:' and 'A short description mentions a {u} and a {v}.
Words so far: {prefix}
Which word should follow? {a} or {b}
Response:'. Keep only single-token leading-space words. For each template select the 256 smallest base-model |q-0.5|, where q is normalized over a,b. Use a constrained two-word readout throughout; unlike the seed's unrestricted interface, this intentionally removes rejection as a confound. Train two teachers from the same base and LoRA initialization: ordinary SciQ SFT U and anchored SFT A. Both use rank/alpha 16 on q_proj,k_proj,v_proj,o_proj,gate_proj,up_proj,down_proj; AdamW, 96 steps, task batch 32, learning rate 1e-4, five-step warmup then linear decay, zero dropout/weight decay and clipping 1. A adds 10 times the mean KL divergence from the cached base Bernoulli(q0) to the current pair-normalized distribution on 16 anchoring prompts per update. U has no anchoring term. Cache only public q0 values. Construct shrinkage comparators U_alpha=M0+alpha*(U-M0), scaling each LoRA product once, for alpha=0,1/16,...,1. Using the lowest-hash 256 validation examples and four-letter-normalized answer NLL, select the alpha whose validation NLL is closest to A's, breaking ties toward smaller alpha. No held-out probe or test outcome may select alpha. Evaluate A, selected U_alpha, unscaled U and M0 on all SciQ test questions and on the 512 held-out-template probes. Report the validation-loss matching error. On probes measure whether constrained greedy a/b choices differ from M0; on SciQ use argmax over the four letter logits. No student extraction model is trained in this MVE.

Use independent repetitions with seeds 17 and 29, each including a freshly trained teacher and four students. Format SciQ without support paragraphs as a four-choice question ending in 'Answer:', with a fixed question-hash permutation of answers. Train each teacher on the lowest-hash 2048 complete training prompts of at most 192 tokens, using full-vocabulary CE on one leading-space correct-letter token. Teacher settings: rank/alpha 16 LoRA on q_proj,k_proj,v_proj,o_proj,gate_proj,up_proj,down_proj, 96 AdamW steps, batch 32, learning rate 1e-4, five-step warmup followed by linear decay, zero dropout/weight decay and clipping 1. Before teacher readout, freeze a public pool generated with RNG 4101: 32768 distinct prompts split equally between 'Question: How might a {u} and a {v} appear in the same short story?' and 'Question: Imagine an ordinary scene containing a {u} and a {v}. What happens next?', each followed by '
Answer: {eight random ordinary words}
Choose exactly one of these two words. Reply only with that word.
Options: {a} | {b}
Answer:'. Use single-token leading-space words from the alphabet. Select 512 prompts per template with smallest base |q-0.5|, with q normalized over the offered pair, yielding 1024 distinct carriers. The readout is explicitly restricted to the offered pair, so no rejection filtering occurs. Fix temperature at 0.8. Breadth policy B receives one independent teacher sample on every carrier. Repetition policy R selects 256 carriers before teacher readout: within each template form eight equal-count public-margin bins and uniformly select 16 carriers per bin; collect four independent samples on every selected carrier. Both policies therefore receive exactly 1024 teacher tokens. For each policy build a public-model control with the same prompts, temperature and number of samples. Couple teacher/public samples using the same independent uniform variate for corresponding draws to reduce comparison variance; do not expose those variates to students. Repeated labels must be fresh draws, not copies of the first draw. It is permissible to cache each model's pair logits once and sample repeatedly from the cached distribution: this exactly simulates independent calls, and the observation budget still counts all returned tokens. Private probabilities are used only by the sampler, never as student targets. Train B_teacher, B_public, R_teacher and R_public from the untouched base, using their 1024 hard-token rows. All four share initial LoRA weights and paired data-order seeds within a repetition. Use rank/alpha 16 on the seven body modules, 60 AdamW steps, effective batch 64, learning rate 5e-5, five-step warmup then linear decay, zero dropout/weight decay and clipping 1. Train full-vocabulary CE only at the final prompt position; retain repeated rows with their independently sampled labels. Evaluate all students, their teacher and the base on the complete SciQ test split, never used for carrier selection or student training. Normalize answer logits over A,B,C,D for NLL and accuracy.

