SETUP. One A100. First command: background downloads (snapshot_download, HF_HUB_ENABLE_HF_TRANSFER=1) of Qwen/Qwen3-8B and Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B while running pip install -U 'transformers>=4.51' peft datasets accelerate scikit-learn hf_transfer and loading datasets. Teacher = Qwen3-8B bf16 (attn_implementation='sdpa'); if its download has not finished 8 minutes after the first command, use Qwen3-4B and record it. Student = Qwen3-0.6B bf16. Both resident on the GPU. Render every chat prompt with apply_chat_template(messages, tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True, enable_thinking=False); batched generation with padding_side='left' and attention mask; 'greedy' = do_sample=False (temperature=None, top_p=None, top_k=None), max_new_tokens 8, batch 64.

TASKS (numpy RandomState(seed); drop rows whose text exceeds 1200 characters; natural class mixes). T1 sms: ucirvine/sms_spam, column sms, 0->ham, 1->spam; shuffle the single train split with seed 0; TEST = first 400 rows; POOL = remaining rows. T2 irony: cardiffnlp/tweet_eval config irony, text, 0->literal, 1->ironic; TEST = 400 from split test (seed 0); POOL = train. T3 subj: SetFit/subj, text, gold = label_text (objective/subjective; verify); TEST = 400 from test; POOL = train. T4 trec: CogComp/trec, text, coarse_label names ABBR, ENTY, DESC, HUM, LOC, NUM (map by name) -> abbreviation, entity, description, human, location, number; TEST = all 500 test rows; POOL = train. T5 rt: cornell-movie-review-data/rotten_tomatoes, text, 0->negative, 1->positive; TEST = 400 from test; POOL = train. T6 agnews: fancyzhx/ag_news, text, 0..3 -> world, sports, business, technology; TEST = 400 from test; POOL = train. Per task: EXEMPLARS = 128 rows sampled from POOL with seed 2 (used only as unlabeled text); SHIFT-REF = 480 further rows sampled from POOL with seed 3, disjoint from EXEMPLARS (used only for the shift diagnostic). Skip a task whose dataset fails to load after one retry (also try the un-namespaced id). Task order as numbered.

SPECS (verbatim). sms: 'Classify an SMS text message as spam or ham. spam = unsolicited commercial or scam messages such as advertisements, prize or lottery notifications, requests to call a number or text a code, or subscription offers. ham = ordinary personal or legitimate messages between people. Output exactly one word: spam or ham.' | irony: 'Decide whether a tweet is ironic or literal. A tweet is ironic when its intended meaning differs from its literal meaning, as in sarcasm, saying the opposite of what is meant, or pointing out a situation whose outcome contradicts expectations; otherwise it is literal. Output exactly one word: ironic or literal.' | subj: 'Decide whether a sentence taken from a movie review or plot summary is subjective or objective. subjective = expresses an opinion, feeling, evaluation, or judgment of the writer. objective = states facts or describes plot events without expressing an opinion. Output exactly one word: subjective or objective.' | trec: 'Classify an English question by the type of answer it asks for: abbreviation (asks what an abbreviation stands for, or for the abbreviation of something), entity (asks for a thing such as an animal, color, event, food, product, substance, symbol, technique, term, or title), description (asks for a definition, description, explanation, reason, or manner), human (asks for a person, group, or organization, or to describe a person), location (asks for a place such as a city, country, mountain, or planet), number (asks for a number, date, count, distance, money amount, percentage, period of time, or other quantity). Output exactly one word: abbreviation, entity, description, human, location, or number.' | rt: 'Classify the sentiment of a one-sentence movie review as positive or negative. Output exactly one word: positive or negative.' | agnews: 'Classify a short news item (a headline followed by the first sentence of the article) into one of four topics: world (international affairs, politics, conflicts), sports, business (companies, markets, economy, finance), technology (science and technology). Output exactly one word: world, sports, business, or technology.'

